WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:02.459
Who do you trust more about COVID?

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:04.319
A legendary rock star or

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:06.539
the world's most popular podcaster?

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.249
The biggest takeaway from the latest

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.139
culture war battle is that you get

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:11.009
to decide.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:12.689
Neil Young pulled his music from

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:14.489
Spotify, but you can still listen to

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:16.349
his discography easily enough.

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:18.149
And even if the world's most popular

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:19.559
streaming service had dropped Joe

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.449
Rogan, he'd still have all sorts

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:22.979
of ways to reach his massive

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:24.749
audience. The scandal du jour

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.119
reminds us that radical free speech

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:28.979
is alive and well.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:31.229
In a profound way, it doesn't

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:32.939
matter if somebody somewhere wants

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:34.589
to block it, because it'll be

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:36.809
available just a few clicks away.

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:38.579
No matter what, Young fired the

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:40.169
first shots by telling the streaming

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:41.789
service Spotify that they had to

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:43.559
choose between keeping his music on

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.389
its platform or continuing to

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:47.219
host Rogan's podcast.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.169
Young says Rogan spreads dangerous

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:50.699
misinformation about COVID.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:52.529
Spotify updated its rules

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:53.969
about publishing content that

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:55.739
promotes dangerous, false or

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:57.449
deceptive medical information about

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:59.699
COVID 19 and other diseases.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:01.529
But it stood squarely by

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:03.629
its marquee star, whose contract

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:06.209
is worth more than a reported $100

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:08.369
million. Young also immediately

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:09.929
inked a deal with satellite radio

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:11.729
provider Sirius, and his music

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:13.949
remains widely available on YouTube,

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:16.079
Apple Music and his own website.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:17.909
Same goes for Joni Mitchell, who

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:19.169
quickly followed suit.

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:21.479
Rogan also keeps rolling on

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.339
telling listeners via Instagram

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:25.229
that the whole point of his show

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:27.299
is to have interesting conversations

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:29.249
and let people come to their own

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:30.119
conclusions.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:31.889
If I pissed you off, I'm sorry.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:33.809
And if you enjoy the podcast,

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:36.029
thank you. Young's call for Spotify

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:38.129
to boot Rogen is risible,

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:40.439
but his decision to pull his music

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:42.749
is worth celebrating as an assertion

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:44.729
of his right not to do business

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:46.379
with an entity that offends.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:48.659
Cancel culture is a real concern,

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:50.609
but its impact is limited by

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:52.709
the limitless nature of how easily

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:54.659
stuff gets made and distributed

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:56.204
these days. Our world of cultural

00:01:56.205 --> 00:01:58.079
plenty was simply

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.389
unimaginable in 1966,

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:02.129
when Neil Young cut his first album

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:03.389
with Buffalo Springfield.

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:05.069
Back then, there were a handful of

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:06.989
major record labels a few movie

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:09.329
studios, three national TV networks,

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:11.339
and only AM and FM radio

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:13.349
gatekeepers actually had a lot

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:14.849
of power to suppress speech they

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:16.139
didn't approve of.

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:17.969
Nowadays, even the mighty

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:20.309
Spotify is just one streaming

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:21.659
service among many.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:23.399
The problem isn't that our choices

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:24.479
are too restricted.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:26.159
It's figuring out how to triage,

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:28.769
what to read, watch and listen to,

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:30.299
because there just aren't enough

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:32.399
hours in the day to take in all

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.499
the amazing movies, shows, songs,

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:36.209
books and podcasts available.

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:38.159
We get to decide what

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:39.959
and when we're going to watch and

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:41.759
who we're going to trust, which is a

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:43.589
great outcome, but one

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:45.389
we should never take for granted.

